{"type": "FeatureCollection", "metadata": {"note": "Yes -- the Louisville-Frankfort-Lexington L&N branch (see ln_louisville_frankfort_lexington.geojson) continued east past Lexington, deep into the Eastern Kentucky coalfields. The Kentucky Union Railway (chartered 1852, but not built out until the 1880s) reached Winchester and Jackson, KY by July 15 1891 -- a company distinct from the L&N at this point. It went into receivership shortly after, and was reorganized as the Lexington & Eastern Railway on Oct 13 1894 (operations resumed Nov 1 1894). The L&N quietly acquired the Lexington & Eastern in Nov 1910 -- kept secret until 1915 to avoid antitrust/coal-monopoly backlash -- and financed a major extension beyond Jackson through Hazard to Fleming (near Neon, KY, close to the Virginia border), completed with 16 bridges across the North Fork Kentucky River by Nov 23 1912. This became the trunk of L&N's 'Eastern Kentucky Division,' the backbone of its coal-hauling business into Harlan/Letcher/Perry county coal country. Passenger service lingered on mail-train usage alone as roads improved; the L&N's last passenger run through Jackson was June 14 1956. Geometry is a real-town waypoint approximation (Lexington-Winchester-Clay City-Beattyville-Jackson-Hazard-Fleming/Neon), straight-line segments between documented stations -- NOT digitized from period track maps.", "sources": ["https://abandonedonline.net/location/kentucky-union-railway/ and the CSXT Historical Society PDF on the Lexington & Eastern (Winchester/Slade reached June 1889; Jackson reached July 15 1891; receivership 1891; reorganized as Lexington & Eastern Railway Oct 13 1894, operations Nov 1 1894; extension with 16 North Fork Kentucky River bridges completed Nov 23 1912)", "https://appalachianrailroadmodeling.com (L&N acquired the Lexington & Eastern Nov 1910, kept secret until 1915; the Eastern Kentucky Division ran La Grange-Fleming, 272 miles; original main went Hazard-Dent-Fleming/Neon; 'Neon' named in Hazard Herald reports from Nov 15 1912, just before the first passenger train reached the town)", "https://bookhiker.com/2023/06/13/last-train-to-jackson/ (L&N filed to end Trains 3 and 4 / all passenger service in late 1955; last passenger run through Jackson June 14 1956, ridership lost to new mountain roads, mail hauling the remaining use)"], "not_yet_sourced": "Exact date the Kentucky Union/Lexington & Eastern's passenger service (as opposed to the later L&N-branded service) began versus construction-complete dates; precise station list east of Jackson beyond Hazard and Fleming/Neon; whether passenger service east of Jackson (Hazard-Fleming) ended at the same June 1956 date as the Jackson segment or separately.", "line_label_audit": "2026-08-24: line labels normalised repo-wide to \"<operating company>: <line name>\" -- the company's common display name (legal suffixes dropped), then the line as a passenger would have known it, or its two terminals where the line carried no advertised name. Long intermediate station lists were reduced to endpoints. For features spanning several operators the company shown is the one that ran the line for most of its drawn years. See CLAUDE.md Rule 6."}, "features": [{"type": "Feature", "properties": {"company": "Kentucky Union Railway / Lexington and Eastern Railway", "line": "Kentucky Union: Lexington \u2013 Jackson", "id": "ky-union-lexington-jackson", "color": "#7A4419", "mode": "intercity_rail", "avg_speed_kmh": 30, "speed_source": "estimated: 1890s steam-era mountain railroad with steep grades and frequent stops; unsourced", "segments": [{"start": 1891, "end": 1912, "headway_min": 720, "note": "Kentucky Union Railway reached Jackson July 15 1891; receivership followed almost immediately, reorganized as the Lexington & Eastern Railway in 1894; frequency estimated/unsourced", "sources": ["NOT SOURCED AT SEGMENT LEVEL: the references below cover this line, but nothing has been checked specifically against this segment's start yea